      "text": "                                                                           WHAT SHARDING SOLVES\n\nEven the largest single database server has limits:\n\n- Maximum storage (finite disk)\n- Maximum write throughput (finite I/O)\n- Maximum CPU for query processing\n- Single point of failure\n\nSharding (also called horizontal partitioning) distributes data across multiple database nodes. Each node holds a subset of the data. Together, \nthey hold all of it.\n\nThe analogy: A library with books organized by author's last name. Books A-F are in Building 1, G-M in Building 2, N-Z in Building 3. No single \nbuilding holds all books, but the system works because you know which building to go to for any author.\n\n                                                        SHARDING VS PARTITIONING - THE TERMINOLOGY\n\nThese terms are often used interchangeably, but there's a subtle distinction:\n\nPartitioning — splitting data within a single database instance. A PostgreSQL table can be partitioned by date (monthly partitions), with all \npartitions on the same server. This helps with query performance and maintenance.\n\nSharding — splitting data across multiple database instances (different servers). True horizontal scaling.\n\nIn system design interviews, people usually mean sharding when they say either term.\n\n                                                                SHARDING STRATEGIES\nSTRATEGY 1: RANGE-BASED SHARDING\n\nData is divided by ranges of the shard key.\n\nUser IDs 1-1,000,000       → Shard 1\nUser IDs 1,000,001-2,000,000 → Shard 2\nUser IDs 2,000,001-3,000,000 → Shard 3\n\nOr by date:\n2024 data → Shard 1\n2025 data → Shard 2\n2026 data → Shard 3\n\nRouting:\n\njavascript\nconst getShard = (userId) => {\n    if (userId <= 1000000) return 'shard1';\n    if (userId <= 2000000) return 'shard2';\n    return 'shard3';\n};\n\nPros:\n\n- Range queries are efficient: \"Get all users with ID between 500,000 and 600,000\" hits only one shard\n- Simple to implement and reason about\n- Natural for time-series data (query last 30 days hits only one or two shards)\n\nCons:\n\n- Hot spots: If most active users have low IDs (they signed up first), Shard 1 handles most traffic while Shards 2 and 3 are idle. This is called \nan uneven distribution or hot shard problem.\n- Rebalancing pain: When Shard 1 fills up, you have to split the range and move data, which is expensive.\n\nBest for: Time-series data (logs, events, metrics) where queries are typically time-bounded. Old data is cold, recent data is hot — a natural \nrange distribution.\n\nSTRATEGY 2: HASH-BASED SHARDING\n\nApply a hash function to the shard key and use the result to determine the shard.\n\njavascript\nconst getShard = (userId, numShards) => {\n    const hash = crc32(userId.toString()); // or MD5, SHA-1, etc.\n    return hash % numShards;\n};\n\n// userId \"usr_123\" → hash → 28374 → 28374 % 4 = 2 → Shard 2\n// userId \"usr_456\" → hash → 91823 → 91823 % 4 = 3 → Shard 3\n\nPros:\n\nEven distribution — data is spread uniformly across shards (good hash functions distribute evenly)\nNo hot spots — any single shard doesn't naturally get more load\nSimple routing logic\n\nCons:\n\nRange queries are expensive: \"Get all users with ID between 1 and 1000\" requires hitting ALL shards because the IDs could be on any shard\nAdding/removing shards remaps everything: If you go from 4 to 5 shards, hash % 5 gives completely different assignments than hash % 4. \nNearly all data needs to move. This is solved by consistent hashing (which has been/will also be discsused in the Distributed Systems section, by God's \ngrace).\n\nBest for: Even read/write distribution where range queries aren't needed. User data accessed by ID. Cache data.\n\nSTRATEGY 3: DIRECTORY-BASED SHARDING\n\nA lookup table (the \"directory\") explicitly maps each key to its shard.\n\n┌─────────────────────────┐\n│         SHARD DIRECTORY          │\n│  userId: usr_123 → Shard 2         │\n│  userId: usr_456 → Shard 1         │\n│  userId: usr_789 → Shard 4         │\n│  userId: usr_012 → Shard 3         │\n│  ...                                        │\n└─────────────────────────┘\n\nRouting:\n\njavascript\nconst getShard = async (userId) => {\n    return await directoryService.lookup(userId);\n};\n\nPros:\n\nMaximum flexibility — you can move individual entities between shards\nNo constraints on distribution — you can assign based on whatever logic makes sense\nEasy to rebalance — just update the directory, not the data\nCan route \"hot\" users to dedicated shards\n\nCons:\n\nThe directory itself is a single point of failure and a bottleneck — every read/write requires a directory lookup\nThe directory must be highly available and low-latency (usually cached heavily)\nMore infrastructure to maintain\n\nBest for: When you need fine-grained control over data placement. Multi-tenant SaaS where you want to keep each customer's data together. \nCases where you know in advance some entities will be much heavier than others.\n\n                                                    CHOOSING A SHARD KEY - THE MOST CRITICAL DECISION\n\nThe shard key determines everything. A bad choice creates hot spots, makes certain queries impossible, or forces expensive cross-shard \noperations.\n\nGood shard key properties:\n\n- High cardinality: Many unique values. A boolean field (true/false) only gives 2 shards maximum.\n- Even distribution: Values distributed roughly uniformly. Not clustered.\n- Aligned with access patterns: The most common queries should be satisfiable with a single shard (and not scatter-gather).\n\nCOMMON SHARD KEYS AND THEIR TRADE-OFFS:\n\nShard Key                                       Pros                                                       Cons\nUser ID                Even distribution (hash), single-user queries efficient              Cross-user queries hit all shards\nTenant ID             All data for one customer on one shard                               Large tenants create hot shards\nGeographic region         Data stays close to users                                 Uneven if users aren't geographically distributed\nDate                    Time-range queries efficient                                            Recent data is always the \"hot\" shard\nContent type           Keeps related data together                                   Uneven if some content types are more popular\n\nTHE MULTI-TENANT EXAMPLE\n\nIf you shard by tenantId, all of Tenant A's data is on Shard 1. Great for Tenant A's queries — they never cross shards. But if Tenant A is \n10x larger than all other tenants combined, Shard 1 is 10x busier. This is the classic hot tenant problem.\n\nSolutions:\n\n- Give large tenants their own dedicated shard\n- Shard large tenants by (tenantId, userId) — data is spread across shards but still grouped by tenant\n- Use directory-based sharding to route large tenants to multiple shards\n\n                                                            THE PAIN OF CROSS-SHARD OPERATIONS\n\nOnce you shard, some operations become fundamentally harder:\n\nCross-shard queries:\n\nsql:\n-- Before sharding: one query\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE created_at > '2026-01-01';\n\n-- After sharding by userId: must query ALL shards and merge\nSELECT * FROM orders_shard1 WHERE created_at > '2026-01-01'\nUNION ALL\nSELECT * FROM orders_shard2 WHERE created_at > '2026-01-01'\nUNION ALL\nSELECT * FROM orders_shard3 WHERE created_at > '2026-01-01';\n\nCross-shard transactions:\nIf User A on Shard 1 transfers money to User B on Shard 2, you need to atomically debit Shard 1 and credit Shard 2. Single-node ACID \ntransactions don't work across shards. You need distributed transactions (covered later or has already been covered in this Distributed Systems \nsection).\n\nCross-shard joins:\nJoining data across shards requires either pulling data from multiple shards to the application layer and joining in memory, or using a distributed \nquery engine.\n\nSchema migrations:\nChanging a column type requires migrating all shards. With 10 shards, you have 10 migrations to run, coordinate, and verify.\n\nThese costs are why sharding is a last resort. Before sharding, exhaust:\n\nVertical scaling (bigger machine)\nRead replicas (distribute read load)\nCaching (reduce database load)\nIndexing (make existing queries faster)\nQuery optimization (fix slow queries)\n\nShard only when all of that is exhausted and you have concrete metrics showing the bottleneck.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "                                                                           WHAT SHARDING SOLVES\n\nEven the largest single database server has limits:\n\n- Maximum storage (finite disk)\n- Maximum write throughput (finite I/O)\n- Maximum CPU for query processing\n- Single point of failure\n\nSharding (also called horizontal partitioning) distributes data across multiple database nodes. Each node holds a subset of the data. Together, \nthey hold all of it.\n\nThe analogy: A library with books organized by author's last name. Books A-F are in Building 1, G-M in Building 2, N-Z in Building 3. No single \nbuilding holds all books, but the system works because you know which building to go to for any author.\n\n                                                        SHARDING VS PARTITIONING - THE TERMINOLOGY\n\nThese terms are often used interchangeably, but there's a subtle distinction:\n\nPartitioning — splitting data within a single database instance. A PostgreSQL table can be partitioned by date (monthly partitions), with all \npartitions on the same server. This helps with query performance and maintenance.\n\nSharding — splitting data across multiple database instances (different servers). True horizontal scaling.\n\nIn system design interviews, people usually mean sharding when they say either term.\n\n                                                                SHARDING STRATEGIES\nSTRATEGY 1: RANGE-BASED SHARDING\n\nData is divided by ranges of the shard key.\n\nUser IDs 1-1,000,000       → Shard 1\nUser IDs 1,000,001-2,000,000 → Shard 2\nUser IDs 2,000,001-3,000,000 → Shard 3\n\nOr by date:\n2024 data → Shard 1\n2025 data → Shard 2\n2026 data → Shard 3\n\nRouting:\n\njavascript\nconst getShard = (userId) => {\n    if (userId <= 1000000) return 'shard1';\n    if (userId <= 2000000) return 'shard2';\n    return 'shard3';\n};\n\nPros:\n\n- Range queries are efficient: \"Get all users with ID between 500,000 and 600,000\" hits only one shard\n- Simple to implement and reason about\n- Natural for time-series data (query last 30 days hits only one or two shards)\n\nCons:\n\n- Hot spots: If most active users have low IDs (they signed up first), Shard 1 handles most traffic while Shards 2 and 3 are idle. This is called \nan uneven distribution or hot shard problem.\n- Rebalancing pain: When Shard 1 fills up, you have to split the range and move data, which is expensive.\n\nBest for: Time-series data (logs, events, metrics) where queries are typically time-bounded. Old data is cold, recent data is hot — a natural \nrange distribution.\n\nSTRATEGY 2: HASH-BASED SHARDING\n\nApply a hash function to the shard key and use the result to determine the shard.\n\njavascript\nconst getShard = (userId, numShards) => {\n    const hash = crc32(userId.toString()); // or MD5, SHA-1, etc.\n    return hash % numShards;\n};\n\n// userId \"usr_123\" → hash → 28374 → 28374 % 4 = 2 → Shard 2\n// userId \"usr_456\" → hash → 91823 → 91823 % 4 = 3 → Shard 3\n\nPros:\n\nEven distribution — data is spread uniformly across shards (good hash functions distribute evenly)\nNo hot spots — any single shard doesn't naturally get more load\nSimple routing logic\n\nCons:\n\nRange queries are expensive: \"Get all users with ID between 1 and 1000\" requires hitting ALL shards because the IDs could be on any shard\nAdding/removing shards remaps everything: If you go from 4 to 5 shards, hash % 5 gives completely different assignments than hash % 4. \nNearly all data needs to move. This is solved by consistent hashing (which has been/will also be discsused in the Distributed Systems section, by God's \ngrace).\n\nBest for: Even read/write distribution where range queries aren't needed. User data accessed by ID. Cache data.\n\nSTRATEGY 3: DIRECTORY-BASED SHARDING\n\nA lookup table (the \"directory\") explicitly maps each key to its shard.\n\n┌─────────────────────────┐\n│         SHARD DIRECTORY          │\n│  userId: usr_123 → Shard 2         │\n│  userId: usr_456 → Shard 1         │\n│  userId: usr_789 → Shard 4         │\n│  userId: usr_012 → Shard 3         │\n│  ...                                        │\n└─────────────────────────┘\n\nRouting:\n\njavascript\nconst getShard = async (userId) => {\n    return await directoryService.lookup(userId);\n};\n\nPros:\n\nMaximum flexibility — you can move individual entities between shards\nNo constraints on distribution — you can assign based on whatever logic makes sense\nEasy to rebalance — just update the directory, not the data\nCan route \"hot\" users to dedicated shards\n\nCons:\n\nThe directory itself is a single point of failure and a bottleneck — every read/write requires a directory lookup\nThe directory must be highly available and low-latency (usually cached heavily)\nMore infrastructure to maintain\n\nBest for: When you need fine-grained control over data placement. Multi-tenant SaaS where you want to keep each customer's data together. \nCases where you know in advance some entities will be much heavier than others.\n\n                                                    CHOOSING A SHARD KEY - THE MOST CRITICAL DECISION\n\nThe shard key determines everything. A bad choice creates hot spots, makes certain queries impossible, or forces expensive cross-shard \noperations.\n\nGood shard key properties:\n\n- High cardinality: Many unique values. A boolean field (true/false) only gives 2 shards maximum.\n- Even distribution: Values distributed roughly uniformly. Not clustered.\n- Aligned with access patterns: The most common queries should be satisfiable with a single shard (and not scatter-gather).\n\nCOMMON SHARD KEYS AND THEIR TRADE-OFFS:\n\nShard Key                                       Pros                                                       Cons\nUser ID                Even distribution (hash), single-user queries efficient              Cross-user queries hit all shards\nTenant ID             All data for one customer on one shard           